+# Hegel property-based testing report — `tera`
+
+Property-based tests added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` v0.28.2) to the
+`tera` crate. All tests live in `tera/tests/basic.rs` inside the `hegel_props` module
+(added alongside the existing integration tests, per the hegel skill).
+
+## 1. What was tested and why
+
+`tera` is a Jinja2-like template engine — an interpreter over untrusted template text.
+The highest-value contracts for such a crate are (a) the compiler must never crash on
+arbitrary input, (b) expression evaluation must match documented arithmetic semantics,
+and (c) HTML autoescaping must never leak an unescaped special character (the classic
+XSS-adjacent bug). Ten live properties plus one pinned reproducer were written:
+
+| # | Property | Kind |
+|---|----------|------|
+| 1 | `compile_arbitrary_text_never_panics` — `add_raw_template` on arbitrary text returns Ok/Err, never panics | no-crash |
+| 2 | `compile_token_soup_never_panics` — same for strings assembled from Tera delimiters/operators (`{{ }}`, `{% %}`, `{# #}`, `\|`, `+`, `(`…) | no-crash |
+| 3 | `deep_guarded_nesting_returns_err_not_overflow` — parens/brackets/blocks nested 50–5000 deep return a clean `Err` (the parser's `MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH` guard) | robustness |
+| 4 | `integer_arithmetic_matches_i128_oracle` — `{{ a + b }}`/`-`/`*` vs Rust `i128::checked_*`: in range → exact decimal, overflow → render error | oracle |
+| 5 | `division_matches_f64_oracle` — `{{ a / b }}` is always float division matching `{:?}`-formatted `f64`; `/0` errors | oracle |
+| 6 | `autoescape_escapes_all_special_chars` — with autoescape on, `{{ x }}` equals a Rust reimplementation of `escape_html`, and no raw `<`,`>`,`"`,`'` survives | autoescape safety |
+| 7 | `safe_filter_is_verbatim` — `{{ x \| safe }}` is byte-for-byte `x` under autoescape | autoescape safety |
+| 8 | `render_is_deterministic` — same template + context → identical output (or identical error) | determinism |
+| 9 | `string_filters_match_rust` — `upper`/`lower`/`length`/`trim` vs `str::to_uppercase`/`to_lowercase`/`chars().count()`/`trim` | oracle |
+| 10 | `undefined_variable_errors` — rendering an absent variable errors, never emits silently (strict undefined) | contract |
+| — | `deep_operator_chain_overflows_stack` — `#[ignore]`d reproducer for the stack-overflow bug (see §3) | pinned bug |
+
+Generators are kept broad: arithmetic draws full-range `i128`/`i64` (hits `MIN`/`MAX`/`0`
+and the overflow boundary); escaping and filter inputs are drawn at the HTML encode-set
+boundary (the five escaped chars, adjacent ASCII, and arbitrary multibyte codepoints),
+which is where an escaper bug would live.
+
+## 2. Oracles used
+
+- **Arithmetic:** transcribed from `tera/src/value/number.rs` — the `math!` macro does
+  `checked_*` in `i128` and errors on overflow; `/` is always `as_float / as_float`.
+  The oracle is Rust's own `i128::checked_add/sub/mul` and `f64` division. This is a
+  genuinely independent computation, not the code under test.
+- **HTML escaping:** transcribed from `tera/src/utils.rs::escape_html` (default,
+  non-`fast_escape` path): `& < > " '` → `&amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &#39;`. Reimplemented
+  character-wise in the test.
+- **String filters:** the Rust standard library (`to_uppercase`, `to_lowercase`,
+  `chars().count()`, `trim`) — `length` uses char count because the `unicode` feature is
+  off by default.
+
+## 3. Bug found: stack overflow on deep unguarded operator/filter chains
+
+**Confirmed, oracle-independent (process abort).** The parser defines
+`MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH = 40` (`tera/src/parsing/parser.rs`) and applies it in
+`inner_parse_expression`, so parentheses, array literals, unary operators and
+`{% if %}`/`{% for %}` nesting all return a graceful *"expression is too complex"* error
+however deep they go (verified up to 100 000). **But left-associative binary chains
+(`1 + 1 + 1 + …`), `and`/`or` chains, and filter chains (`1 | abs | abs | …`) do not go
+through that guard.** Compiling such a template aborts the whole process with a stack
+overflow.
+
+- Threshold: safe at 8000 repetitions, **aborts at ~9000** (measured; between 8000 and
+  9000). The abort happens *inside* `add_raw_template` (compile time), before any render.
+- Reproducer: `hegel_props::deep_operator_chain_overflows_stack` builds
+  `{{ 1 + 1 + … }}` with 9000 terms and is `#[ignore]`d, because a stack overflow is a
+  `SIGABRT` that cannot be caught and would take the whole test binary down with it.
+  Running it with `--ignored` reproduces `fatal runtime error: stack overflow, aborting`
+  (SIGABRT).
+- Impact: any application that compiles untrusted template text (exactly the
+  `Tera::one_off` use case the docs describe) can be crashed by a moderately long flat
+  expression. The existing guard shows the maintainers intend to protect against this
+  class; the binary-operator / filter-chain paths are simply not covered by it.
+- The live no-panic properties (#2, #3) cap generated depth well below the threshold so
+  they stay green and keep exercising everything else; the overflow is pinned solely by
+  the `#[ignore]`d reproducer. **No source code was changed** (per the task's Run-and-
+  Reflect instruction).
+
+## 4. How to run
+
+```bash
+# The 10 live properties (default 100 cases each):
+cargo test -p tera --test basic hegel_props
+
+# The documented stack-overflow reproducer (aborts the process — run alone):
+cargo test -p tera --test basic hegel_props::deep_operator_chain_overflows_stack -- --ignored
+```
+
+Setup: `cargo add --dev hegeltest -p tera` (already applied to `tera/Cargo.toml`).
+`.hegel/` is git-ignored.
+
+## 5. Confidence / validation notes
+
+- **Liveness confirmed by mutation.** Inverting each oracle (e.g. `checked_add` →
+  `checked_sub` for `+`, dropping `<` from the escape oracle, `to_uppercase` →
+  `to_lowercase`) made hegel report a shrunk counterexample for every mutated property,
+  so none of them pass vacuously.
+- **High-count exploratory pass.** All ten live properties were run once at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10×); all passed (wall time scaled ~10×, confirming the extra
+  cases actually ran), then reverted to the default count for the committed tests.
+- **Bug severity.** The stack overflow is oracle-independent (a process abort on
+  untrusted input reaching a checked entry point), so confidence is high that it is a
+  real defect and not an artefact of the test.
+- The eval and filter oracles are transcriptions of the crate's own documented
+  semantics; they passed at 1000 cases across the full `i128`/`f64` range and full
+  Unicode strings, so no arithmetic or escaping disagreement was found beyond the crate's
+  documented behavior.
